Now I have the full picture. All existing tests use a non-empty `workCountries` (BRAZIL), except for the "residence question" test (lines 180-192). This one passes `{ location: "Remote - USA", ...BRAZIL }` but is handled by the earlier RESIDENCE_RE branch, and never reaches `whereTheWorkIs` at all. In other words, none of the existing tests pin down the empty `workCountries` -> "yes" behavior.
My fix in `whereTheWorkIs`:
```ts
function whereTheWorkIs(job: JobReach): "yes" | "no" | "unknown" {
// No work country on file, no evidence for a positive answer - the
// negative one does not assert anything.
if (!job.workCountries.length) return "unknown";
const places = namedPlaces(job.location, job.remoteScope);
if (places.countries.length) {
return places.countries.some((c) => job.workCountries.includes(c))
? "yes"
: "no";
}
if (places.open) return "yes";
return reachAllows(places, job.workCountries) === "yes" ? "yes" : "unknown";
}
```
Hmm, wait - one thing to consider about `if (places.open) return "yes"` when workCountries is non-empty: this means "the posting hires anywhere -> the candidate works from home -> is authorized where they live". This requires the candidate to have a work country (i.e., a place where they can work). With my reordering, workCountries being non-empty is guaranteed, so "open -> yes" is now backed by evidence. Good - and this is exactly the rationale that the existing tests at lines 131-142 and 171-178 pin down.
Also, the doc comment for `whereTheWorkIs` (lines 121-138) should be updated too, to record the empty-profile rule. The comment on lines 136-138 says "Therefore: a named country decides, and only postings that name nothing fall back to 'hires anywhere', which means the candidate works from home and is authorized there." I'll add a sentence: "When nothing is on file about where the candidate can work, no positive answer is available, and the answer is the negative one."
